{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he said to HaShem: Turn not, etc.</b> The intention is that because he saw the extraordinary hatred the wicked bore toward him, from this he recognized that they were of the aspect of absolute evil, whose nature is to hate the aspect of good with a hatred of death without cause. Therefore he said to HaShem: <b>Turn not to their offering.</b> And the intention in this is that there is no person who does not have in his hand some mitzvot and good deeds, and it has already preceded us that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not diminish the reward of any creature, even that of the wicked, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:4): ״אֵל אֱמוּנָה וְאֵין עָוֶל״, and go out and learn from the reward for the footsteps of that wicked one (Sanhedrin 96a). Therefore Moses was concerned that some mitzvah in their hand might stand as a merit that protects them; for this he sought from HaShem and said: <b>Turn not to their offering</b> — meaning, even a merit that has already reached the status of an offering to HaShem, He should not turn to it nor look upon it." "<b>And if</b> you will say: How could He override, as it were, His attribute for those men to pervert their judgment?" "<b>Know</b> that the righteous have the power to nullify the merit found among the wicked when they see that they are resolved in evil, and this is the sod of (II Samuel 23:3): ״צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹהִים״ — meaning, he rules over the fear of Elohim that a person performs: if it appears proper in their eyes to nullify his merit from him, they have the power to do so, which the Holy One, blessed be He, does not do, as it were. And you will find an analogous case in earthly law, that a court can declare the property of a wicked person ownerless, as they said (Gittin 36b): ״הֶפְקֵר בֵּית דִּין הֶפְקֵר״." "And if you will say: Why do you, Moses, need to intensify the punishment of these men? If it is because of their hatred toward him, that alone would not obligate such severe punishment. Furthermore, the prosecuting party could raise a concern here and say: A person is not suspected of a matter unless there is something in him (Mo'ed Katan 18b), and if he does not have all of it, he has part of it. Therefore he stated before HaShem a sufficient reason to obligate them, and he began by removing any ground for suspicion and said: <b>Not one donkey</b>, etc. — meaning, they have no grounds to suspect him of lust for rulership, for the way of kings is to take their needs from the people, particularly in matters that are for the people's benefit, and he did not take even one donkey that he needed to ride upon to redeem them. If so, what grounds do they have to suspect him of lust for rulership?" "<b>And he added</b> to say <b>nor have I harmed</b>, etc. — meaning, you might say that they bear hatred toward me for some cause that I brought upon them in some matter customary in the world, that things occur among people from which enmities are drawn — <b>nor have I harmed</b>, etc., meaning, from my days I have never done them harm from which hatred toward me would be drawn on its account." "There is a fine point to examine in his saying <b>et one of them</b>: the meaning is, I did not cause harm even in the drawing of evil from one of them to another, as when one might say — they did not come before me for judgment and I convicted the innocent or acquitted the guilty — thereby harming one of them, meaning that when one obligates money from one to give to the other, the evil is drawn to him from the other who takes from him what he does not owe him. And even this he did not do, on the contrary, for he judged with the prophecy of true judgment to the utmost truth, as they said, of blessed memory (Zohar vol. II 78a) on the verse ״כִּי יִהְיֶה לָהֶם דָּבָר בָּא אֵלַי״. From this it follows that there is no cause for their hatred and suspicion in any manner whatsoever; it is only because they are an utterly evil portion, and the nature of evil is to hate good with intense natural hatred without cause. From this it follows that in all similar cases it is a mitzvah to uproot them and to invalidate their merit, so that HaShem will not turn to their offering, for בַּאֲבֹד רְשָׁעִים רִנָּה (when the wicked perish, there is song). And I am inclined to think that these wicked men are not included in the assembly of Korah, about whom it was said (Sanhedrin 108a) ״מוֹרִיד שְׁאוֹל וַיָּעַל״ (He brought the living down to Sheol), for these — their hope is lost, for they are all a branch of the klipah. So may all the enemies of HaShem perish."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:25.311052039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}